# ${{APP_NAME}}

An HTTP server in TypeScript on Node.js's built-in `node:http`, created with Code on the Go.

## Run

Tap **Run app**, then open <http://localhost:${{PORT}}> in a browser on this device. Tap
**Cancel Run app** to stop the server. From the terminal:

```bash
npm start
```

Set the `PORT` environment variable to listen on another port.

## Routes

| Method | Path                     | Response                        |
| ------ | ------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| GET    | `/`                      | a plain-text greeting           |
| GET    | `/api/hello?name=Ada`    | `{ "message": "Hello, Ada!" }`  |
|        | anything else            | `404` with a JSON error         |

## Test

Tap **Run tests**, or `npm test`. The tests start the server on a free port and call it with
`fetch`, so they need no network connection.

## Project structure

```
${{APP_NAME}}/
  package.json        # name, scripts, and the Node.js type declarations
  tsconfig.json       # strict type checking, no output files
  src/server.ts       # starts the server and shuts it down cleanly on SIGINT and SIGTERM
  src/app.ts          # the request handler and its routes
  src/app.test.ts     # tests
```
